# Every path rule here is anchored with a leading slash so it applies to THIS
# repository's own output and nothing else. An unanchored `out/` matches `out/`
# at any depth, including inside a captured upstream tree under `references/`,
# which is how 20 files of somebody else's committed instrument output were
# silently dropped from every clone (correction 23).
# `scripts/check-corpus-integrity.py` is the gate that keeps this true.
#
# The corollary is that a rule copied from anywhere is anchored before it is
# added. `.gitignore` also only applies to files git is not ALREADY tracking,
# so adding a line never untracks anything. Check with `git ls-files`.

# -- credentials. Listed before any such file exists, so one can never be
# -- staged by accident. A rule added after the fact leaves a window.
/.env
/.env.*
/.dev.vars
/.dev.vars.*
*.pem
*.key
*.p12
*.pfx
/credentials.json
/service-account*.json
*.token

# -- Python
__pycache__/
*.pyc
/.venv/
/.mypy_cache/
/.ruff_cache/
/.pytest_cache/

# -- this project's build output
/out/
/out.staging/
/out.previous/
/coverage/
/.coverage

# -- session-local scratch (inputs, working notes). Never part of the
# -- published tree, and never where evidence is kept: nothing untracked
# -- survives a session. docs/methodology/references.md section 4.
/.tmp/

# -- operating system and editor noise
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db
Desktop.ini
desktop.ini
/.vscode/
/.idea/
*.swp
*~

# -- agent-local settings, which are the contributor's and not the project's
/.claude/settings.local.json
/CLAUDE.local.md

# -- Windows reserved device names. Two different things create one as a real
# -- file and only the first is something you wrote: a `2>/dev/null` redirect
# -- under a shell that does not map it, and a tool passing `NUL` as a filename
# -- argument. A tracked one breaks `git stash` and cannot be removed by `rm`.
/nul
/NUL
/CON
/PRN
/AUX
/COM[1-9]
/LPT[1-9]

# ⛔ `references/` is NOT ignored, and that is deliberate. The corpus is the
# evidence: a conclusion nobody can re-check is an opinion, and a sweep that
# kept its data in an ignored directory lost it entirely.
# docs/methodology/references.md.
